! jb_af1c F219 "NOISY MODE TEST ROUTINE", sub-blocks K through P2,
! pp.171-172 (octal 021537.40-021573.00): compare magnitude for range,
! multiply, divide, reciprocal divide, store root, and add double.
!
! F219A carried A through J and left off at 021537.40 with noisy mode
! STILL ON -- the routine sets it once and every block from there on
! inherits it.  This program picks up at exactly that address and
! re-establishes the same state at its own start.
!
! WHAT THIS BLOCK IS FOR.  A-J covered add, add to magnitude, load,
! load with flag, add to memory, store and the three compares.  K-P2
! reach the operations A-J never touched under noisy mode: MULTIPLY,
! DIVIDE, RECIPROCAL DIVIDE, STORE ROOT and ADD DOUBLE.  Every one of
! them was implemented before F219 and every one of them passes `noisy`
! into the model's FP functions -- but nothing had ever RUN them with
! noisy set, so whether that plumbing is correct is untested.
!
! THE RULE BEING TESTED.  p.87: noisy mode enters ones, not zeros, into
! the low-order positions vacated by left-shift normalization; and, for
! ADD only, the operand with the HIGH EXPONENT is pre-extended with 48
! low-order one bits before the intermediate sum is formed.  The
! interesting question for K-P2 is what that means for an operation
! whose intermediate result is already 96 bits wide, where the vacated
! positions are in a low half the 48-bit answer discards.
!
! F219K  COMPARE MAGNITUDE FOR RANGE.  F219J did KR; this is KMGR, the
!        magnitude form, on the same pair (FK124 loaded double
!        unnormalized, compared against FPK32) and expecting the same
!        AH.  The BB1 sets AH first, deliberately -- it is checking that
!        a compare-for-range LEAVES AH set, not that it sets it.  NM is
!        untouched here, unlike F219H/I, which cleared $IND and had to
!        put noisy mode back.
!
! F219L  MULTIPLY.  FPK52 = +0.29cbb829cbb9 at +2 loaded unnormalized,
!        times FPK80 = +0.800000000000 at +2, normalized.  The 48x48
!        product is EXACT in 96 bits; the sum of exponents is +4 and the
!        product 0x29cbb829cbb9/2^49 has three leading zeros, so
!        normalization shifts left three and the exponent lands at +1.
!        0x29cbb829cbb9 << 2 = 0xa72ee0a72ee4, which is FK118's fraction
!        exactly, at FK118's exponent of +1.  The three positions noisy
!        mode fills with ones are at the BOTTOM of the 96-bit product,
!        forty-five places below the 48-bit answer, so the expected
!        value is the clean product and the block is asserting that
!        noisy mode does not leak upward.
!
! F219M  DIVIDE.  FK95 = +0.1fffffffffff at +8 over FK96 = the same
!        fraction at +6.  The quotient fraction is exactly 1.0, which is
!        not a representable normalized fraction, so it comes out as
!        +0.800000000000 with the exponent one higher: 8-6+1 = +3, which
!        is FPK74.  An exact division with a zero remainder: nothing is
!        vacated, so again the expectation is the clean quotient.
!
! F219N  RECIPROCAL DIVIDE.  The same two constants the other way round
!        through R/, which divides the MEMORY operand by the
!        accumulator, so the quotient is the same and the expected value
!        is FPK74 again.  The pair L,/ and L,R/ with the operands
!        swapped is the standard way this diagnostic shows that an
!        operation follows its named operand and not its position.
!
! F219O  STORE ROOT.  FK81 = +0.100000000000 at +10, whose square root
!        is +0.800000000000 at +5 -- but the listing's own comment says
!        what is really being checked: "CHECK NO LOW ORDER ONE BITS IN
!        RESULT".  SRT stores the root to ROS; the L(BU,64) of FK82 and
!        the CT against ROS at offset 4 compare SIXTY bits starting four
!        bits in, which is the fraction and the sign and NOT the flag
!        byte.  A root is developed one bit at a time and terminates
!        exactly here, so a noisy implementation that filled the tail
!        would show up immediately.
!
! F219P1 ADD DOUBLE, exponent of memory equal to exponent of
!        accumulator, both extended by 48.  FPK38 is loaded double
!        unnormalized and its own fraction written into the
!        accumulator's low half by the C0011, so the accumulator holds a
!        96-bit value whose halves are the same; D+ then adds FPK35.
!        The two CT lines check the accumulator's high half against
!        FPK99 and its low half against FPK32 -- a double-precision
!        answer needs both halves checked, which single-precision
!        blocks like F219A never had to do.
!
! F219P2 The same sum with the operands swapped, and this time WITHOUT
!        the C0011: the accumulator's low half is whatever DL(U) of
!        FPK35 left there.  High half against FPK99 again, low half
!        against FPK38.

! -- SSIP framework substitutes: SERS (octal 1304) -> FAIL halt,
! -- SEN (octal 1310) -> scratch.
        SLC, (8)1304.0
SERS    BEW, $
        SLC, (8)1310.0
SEN     DD(BU,64,8), 0

! -- constants (pools pp.176-179) --
        SLC, (8)22045.0
FPK32   DD(BU,64,8), (8)0001177777777777777760
        SLC, (8)22050.0
FPK35   DD(BU,64,8), (8)0001125252525252525240
        SLC, (8)22053.0
FPK38   DD(BU,64,8), (8)0031052525252525252520
        SLC, (8)22071.0
FPK52   DD(BU,64,8), (8)0001024713560247135620
        SLC, (8)22115.0
FPK74   DD(BU,64,8), (8)0001500000000000000000
        SLC, (8)22121.0
FPK80   DD(BU,64,8), (8)0001100000000000000000
        SLC, (8)22140.0
        DD(BU,64,8), (8)0030525252525252525320
FPK99   DD(BU,64,8), (8)0000525252525252525260
        SLC, (8)22262.0
FK81    DD(BU,64,8), (8)0005010000000000000000
FK82    DD(BU,64,8), (8)0002100000000000000000
        SLC, (8)22300.0
FK95    DD(BU,64,8), (8)0004017777777777777760
FK96    DD(BU,64,8), (8)0003017777777777777760
        SLC, (8)22327.0
FK118   DD(BU,64,8), (8)0000523456701234567100
        SLC, (8)22335.0
FK124   DD(BU,64,8), (8)0002417777777777777760
        SLC, (8)22355.0
ROS     DD(BU,64,8), 0

! One half-word before the block, so that F219K's own first
! instruction lands on 021537.40 exactly and every address below
! matches the listing column: F219 sets noisy mode once, back in
! F219A, and this program has to re-establish it.
        SLC, (8)21536.40
SAP25   BB1, $NM, $+1.0                      ! noisy mode is on for all of F219
! -- COMPARE MAGNITUDE FOR RANGE CHECK --
! F219K
        B, $+.32
        BB1, $AH, $+1.0                      ! set AH: a range compare must leave it set
        DL(U), FK124
        KMGR(N), FPK32
        SIC, SEN
        BZAH, SERS                           ! error if AH off
! -- MULTIPLY CHECK --
! F219L
        B, $+.32
        L(U), FPK52
        *(N), FPK80
        CT0110(BU,60), FK118, 68             ! the clean product: noisy ones stay low
        SIC, SEN
        BRGZ, SERS
! -- DIVIDE CHECK --
! F219M
        B, $+.32
        L(U), FK95
        /(N), FK96
        CT0110(BU,60), FPK74, 68
        SIC, SEN
        BRGZ, SERS
! -- RECIPROCAL DIVIDE CHECK --
! F219N
        B, $+.32
        L(U), FK96
        R/(N), FK95
        CT0110(BU,60), FPK74, 68
        SIC, SEN
        BRGZ, SERS
! -- STORE ROOT CHECK --
! F219O
        B, $+.32
        L(U), FK81
        SRT(N), ROS
        L(BU,64), FK82
        CT0110(BU,60), ROS, 4                ! check no low order one bits in result
        SIC, SEN
        BRGZ, SERS
! -- ADD DOUBLE CHECK --
! F219P1 - exp mem + exp acc + +48
        DL(U), FPK38
        C0011(BU,48), FPK38+.12, 20          ! set the accumulator's low half
        D+(N), FPK35
        CT0110(BU,48), FPK99+.12, 68         ! high half
        SIC, SEN
        BRGZ, SERS
        CT0110(BU,48), FPK32+.12, 20         ! low half
        SIC, SEN
        BRGZ, SERS
! F219P2 - the same pair, sides swapped, no C0011
        DL(U), FPK35
        D+(N), FPK38
        CT0110(BU,48), FPK99+.12, 68         ! high half
        SIC, SEN
        BRGZ, SERS
        CT0110(BU,48), FPK38+.12, 20         ! low half
! The SIC/BRGZ that tests this last compare is printed at the TOP of
! p.172, above F219P3 -- the listing's rows are not in address order
! there.  Reading only the bottom of the page would leave P2's low
! half compared and never tested, which is the same shape of hole as
! a constant checked by a block that cannot see one of its fields.
        SIC, SEN
        BRGZ, SERS
PASS    BEW, $
        END, SAP25
